          %p
            which is probably the best known mathematical formula for entropy, due to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Max+Planck"} Max Planck
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ludwig+Boltzmann"} Ludwig Boltzmann
            %a{:href => "#physical"} physical interpretation
            appears below.)
          %p
            Of all probability measures on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/maximum+entropy"} maximum entropy
          %h3#entropy_with_respect_to_an_absolutely_continuous_probability_measure_on_the_real_line Entropy with respect to an absolutely continuous probability measure on the real line
          %p
            Recall that a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borel+measure"} Borel measure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            on an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interval"} interval
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/real+line"} real line
            is
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/absolutely+continuous+measure"} absolutely continuous
            if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(A) = 0
            whenever
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/null+set"} null set
            (with respect to
            = succeed ")," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lebesgue+measure"} Lebesgue measure
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo &gt;
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(A) \gt 0
            whenever the Lebesgue measure of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is positive. In this case, we can take the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Radon%E2%80%93Nikodym+derivative"} Radon–Nikodym derivative
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            with respect to Lebesgue measure, to get an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integrable+function"} integrable function
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            = succeed ";" do
              %strong probability distribution function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu
            by
          #eq:pdf.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (9)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi A
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu(A) = \int_A f(x) \mathrm{d}x ,
          %p where the integral is taken with respect to Lebesgue measure.
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒫
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}
            is a partition (or a Lebesgue-almost-partition) of an interval
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            into
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Borel+sets"} Borel sets
            %strong entropy
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒫
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}
            with respect to an integrable function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is the entropy of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒫
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{P}
            with respect to the measure induced by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            using the integral formula
            = succeed ";" do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:pdf"} (9)
          #eq:EqF.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (10)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝒫
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi A
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi 𝒫
                  %mi log
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} (
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi A
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %mi x
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mo ∫
                        %mi A
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                      %mi x
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  H_f(\mathcal{P})
                  \=
                  \-\sum_{A \in \mathcal{P}}
                  \\log\big(
                  (\int_A f(x) \mathrm{d}x)^{\int_A f(x) \mathrm{d}x}
                  \\big)
                  \\,.
          %p
            On the other hand, the
            %strong entropy
            of the probability distribution space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X,f)
            is the entropy of the entire
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %em not
            generated by a partition) with respect to
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
          #eq:EqG.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (11)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msub
                    %mo ∫
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi X
                  %mi log
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi f
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi x
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} d
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  H_f(X) = -\int_{x \in X} \log(f(x)^{f(x)}) \mathrm{d}x
          %p by a fairly complicated argument.
          .query
            %p I haven't actually managed to check this argument yet, although my memory tags it as a true fact. —Toby
          %h3#entropy_of_a_density_matrix Entropy of a density matrix
          %p
            In the analogy between
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+physics"} classical physics
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/density+operators"} density operators
            on a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
          %p
            So just as the entropy of a probability distribution
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            is given by
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ∫
                    %mi f
                    %mi log
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} - \int f \log f
            %strong entropy
            of a density operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho
            is
          #eq:EqH.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (12)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi H
                    %mi ρ
                  %mo ≔
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi Tr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ρ
                  %mi log
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  H_\rho \coloneqq -Tr (\rho \log \rho)
                  \\,.
          %p
            using the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+calculus"} functional calculus
          %p
            These are both special cases of the entropy of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state"} state
            on a
            = succeed "-" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^*
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/C-star-algebra"} algebra
          %p
            There is a way to fit this into the framework given by
            = succeed "," do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:general"} (4)
          %h3#RelativeEntropy Relative entropy
          %p
            For two finite probability distributions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi q
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} q
            %strong relative entropy
            is
          #eq:EqJ.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (13)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi q
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %munderover
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo =
                      %mn 1
                    %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi log
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi k
                  %mo −
                  %mi log
                  %msub
                    %mi q
                    %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S(p/q) \coloneqq \sum_{k = 1}^n p_k(log p_k - log q_k)
                  \\,.
          %p
            Or alternatively, for
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \rho, \phi
            two
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/density+matrix"} density matrices
          #eq:EqK.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (14)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi tr
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi log
                  %mi ρ
                  %mo −
                  %mi log
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S(\rho/\phi) \coloneqq tr \rho(log \rho - log \phi)
                  \\,.
          %p
            There is a generalization of these definitions to
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/state"} state
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/von+Neumann+algebra"} von Neumann algebra
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Araki"} Araki
          %p
            For more on this see
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relative+entropy"} relative entropy
          %h2#physical Physical entropy
          %p
            As hinted above, any probability distribution on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/phase+space"} phase space
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/classical+physics"} classical physics
            has an entropy, and any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/density+matrix"} density matrix
            on a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+physics"} quantum physics
            has an entropy. However, these are
            = succeed "," do
              %strong microscopic entropy
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thermodynamics"} thermodynamics
            and most other branches of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
          %p
            Instead, physicists use
            %em coarse-grained
            entropy, which corresponds mathematically to taking the entropy of a
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            microscopic degrees of freedom, we identify
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            macroscopic degrees of freedom that we can reasonably expect to measure, giving a map from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^N
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            (or more generally, a map from an
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pulls back
            to a
            = succeed "-algebra" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^N
            %strong macroscopic entropy
            of a statistical state is the
            %span.newWikiWord
              conditional entropy
              %a{:href => "/nlab/new/conditional+entropy"} ?
            of this
            = succeed "-algebra." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} N
            is on the order of
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Avogadro%27s+number"} Avogadro's number
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            is rarely more than half a dozen, and often as small as
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
          %p
            If we specify a state by a point in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fibre"} fibre
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℝ
                      %mi N
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^N
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/maximum+entropy"} maximum entropy
            = succeed "." do
              %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:Boltzmann"} (8)
          %p
            A more sophisticated approach (pioneered by
            = succeed ")" do
              %span.newWikiWord
                Josiah Gibbs
                %a{:href => "/nlab/new/Josiah+Gibbs"} ?
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mi N
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^N
            of pure microstates) whose
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/expectation+values"} expectation values
            of total energy and other
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/extensive+quantities"} extensive quantities
            (among those that are functions of the macrostate) match the given pure macrostate (point in
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
